        // So we cannot create a contract that reverts. 
        // However, we can make the call fail by exceeding gas? Not controllable.
        // But note: The contract C has a fallback that increments depositsCount. That consumes gas. Not relevant.
        // Another idea: the call can fail if the target does not have a payable fallback and we send value > 0. But any EOA without code will accept ether. That succeeds.
        // Actually we can target the contract itself? sendMoney to itself? call{value:...}(address(this))? That would call the contract's fallback, which is payable and increments depositsCount. That succeeds.
        // To make .call return false, we need the target's code to revert. Without being able to deploy such a contract, we are stuck.
        // Wait: there is a special case: call to address(0) with value > 0? In Solidity 0.8+, sending ether to address(0) always succeeds. So that returns true.
        // So no way to get false from .call under allowed cheatcodes.
        // But the difference statement explicitly says: 'after a call to sendMoney(...) completes, the value of state variable sendMoney differs: P.sendMoney() != M.sendMoney()'. Ignoring the misnomer 'sendMoney', perhaps they mean the function sendMoney changes some state differently. Actually, look at the reference code: sendMoney does not modify any state variable of C. So the only state that could differ is the balance of a target or of the contract? But balances are also state. The contract's balance: in reference, if call fails, require reverts so contract balance unchanged; in bug, call fails but no revert, contract balance also unchanged (since no transfer). So same. The target's balance: if call fails, no transfer, so same. So no difference in any observable state except the revert boolean.
        // Therefore the only way to observe divergence is through a try/catch: in the test, we call c.sendMoney and catch revert. In reference it reverts, in bug it doesn't.
